<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 47 0 R
/T (Abstract This report presents a comprehensive exploratory data analysis \(EDA\) of a large-scale global salary survey dataset consisting of over 28,000 individual responses. The dataset captures demographic, educational, occupational, and geographic information alongside reported annual compensation figures. The primary objective of this analysis is to understand salary distributions, identify patterns and disparities across demographic and professional attributes, and establish a reliable analytical foundation for future downstream modeling or policy-oriented research.)
/E (Abstract This report presents a comprehensive exploratory data analysis \(EDA\) of a large-scale global salary survey dataset consisting of over 28,000 individual responses. The dataset captures demographic, educational, occupational, and geographic information alongside reported annual compensation figures. The primary objective of this analysis is to understand salary distributions, identify patterns and disparities across demographic and professional attributes, and establish a reliable analytical foundation for future downstream modeling or policy-oriented research.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
66 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 47 0 R
/T (A rigorous data preparation workflow is implemented, including data type corrections, handling of missing  values, extensive categorical standardization, and outlier detection using the interquartile range \(IQR\)  method. Particular attention is devoted to harmonizing free-text country and state entries, simplifying  ethnicity categories, and resolving inconsistencies in work experience descriptors.)
/E (A rigorous data preparation workflow is implemented, including data type corrections, handling of missing  values, extensive categorical standardization, and outlier detection using the interquartile range \(IQR\)  method. Particular attention is devoted to harmonizing free-text country and state entries, simplifying  ethnicity categories, and resolving inconsistencies in work experience descriptors.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
67 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 47 0 R
/T (The exploratory phase examines univariate, bivariate, and multivariate relationships through descriptive  statistics and visualizations. Salary distributions are analyzed across industries, education levels, job titles,  gender, ethnicity, geographic regions, and experience bands. The results highlight substantial compensation  heterogeneity driven by industry specialization, seniority, and geography. Technology, energy, and  engineering sectors consistently exhibit higher median salaries, while service-oriented and nonprofit fields  occupy lower compensation bands.)
/E (The exploratory phase examines univariate, bivariate, and multivariate relationships through descriptive  statistics and visualizations. Salary distributions are analyzed across industries, education levels, job titles,  gender, ethnicity, geographic regions, and experience bands. The results highlight substantial compensation  heterogeneity driven by industry specialization, seniority, and geography. Technology, energy, and  engineering sectors consistently exhibit higher median salaries, while service-oriented and nonprofit fields  occupy lower compensation bands.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
